Subsample experiment, 15-Sep-2026: does agreement between search partitions at neighbouring rungs, and
between seeds at one rung, peak where the partition scores best against the CDC types?

Subsets
  vp1s300, rdrps300: 300 names each, drawn uniformly at random from the labelled rows of
  NailIt/test/data/denovo/{vp1,rdrp}.cdc with `shuf -n 300 --random-source=<(yes 17)`; the names are
  in {vp1,rdrp}s300.sel and the labels in labels/{vp1,rdrp}s300.cdc.  VP1 has 24 CDC types in the
  subset, RdRp 29.  Databases built from tmp/regions/{vp1,rdrp} with
  `KPopCountDB -T 1 -i <full> -L <names> -N -D -o <subset>`; --summary shows 300 spectra each.

Runs (subsample-run)
  Frozen stage-1 binary (tmp/stage1/runs/KPop-autotuner, copied into runs/).  Calibrated detector,
  --dimensions fixed at 4, 8, 16, 32 and 64, seeds 17, 18 and 19, -T 1, nice 19, two at a time,
  --iterations 8 --montecarlo-steps 1000 --montecarlo-replicas 4 --montecarlo-level finest.
  Projection and partition samples of 68: the autotuner's automatic size for 300 spectra, leaving 67
  axes, so every rung up to 64 fits.  RdRp is the calibration: on the full corpus its good range is
  16-32 axes, where the neighbouring-rung and cross-seed ARI also peaked.

Choices made here, not by the user
  - uniform sampling, not stratified by type (rare types may be absent or singletons);
  - shuf seed 17;
  - samples of 68 rather than a fixed 200, so the sample is the same fraction of the corpus as the
    autotuner would take by itself;
  - rung 128 left out, since 67 axes are available.

Outcomes as they came
  rdrps300.fixed4.s17 refused after 22s (exit 2): at 4 axes the calibrated detector keeps 1 of 5
  troughs, with 58.6% of pairs below it (z 7.1), so there are "no groups at a usable level".  This
  is the designed stop, not a crash; the protocol was not changed (no --report-anyway), and the
  refusal is the outcome at that rung.
  rdrps300.fixed4.s19 refused in round 2 (57s) and rdrps300.fixed4.s18 in round 3 (91s), for the
  same reason: their earlier rounds had kept valleys at 23-27% of pairs, but the re-twisted embedding
  kept only valleys above half (53.9%) or none (0 of 3).  So at 4 axes all three seeds refuse, and
  the stop can come in a later round, after partitions were printed; the run's exit status is 2
  either way and stage1-score reports it as failed.
  rdrps300.fixed8.s18 refused in round 7 (363s) after six sensible rounds (11-22 clusters, one kept
  valley each at 13-15% of pairs, z 3.3-7.2): round 7 kept 0 of 5 troughs.  rdrps300.fixed8.s19
  refused in round 2 (79s): round 1 kept one valley at 21.7% (z 3.8), round 2 kept 0 of 6.  On the
  full corpus the kept valleys had z 20-28; here they sit near the threshold, so a round loses its
  only valley by chance.  --report-anyway is not a clean way round: a round with no usable valley
  then starts its search from singletons (radius 0, Clustering.run_montecarlo), a different search.
  rdrps300.fixed8.s17 finished all 8 rounds (459s; 9-19 clusters; main valley 12.5-22.5%, z 3.3-9.8).
  rdrps300.fixed16.s17 refused in round 1 (28s): 0 of 4 troughs kept.

Why the refusals: z scales with the square root of the corpus
  The same RdRp valley (8-15% of pairs, reappearing in 100% of resamples) has z 22-30 in most rounds
  of the full-corpus controls (control/rdrp.fixed{8,16,32}.s17.stderr) and z 3.3-9.8 here.  The ratio,
  about 0.3, is sqrt(300/3228) = 0.305: a significance grows with the number of spectra, so at 300
  the valley sits near the keep threshold (valley_z_min = 3, Clustering.ml) and a round loses it by
  chance, which the run treats as no groups.  The subsample shrinks the evidence, not only the cost.
