Partial results, 29 of 30 runs (subsample-compare into partial/, 22:05)
  Label scores, median over the rounds 5-8 each run printed (homogeneity/completeness):
    RdRp  8 axes: s17 0.903/0.936, s18 0.922/0.887 (2 rounds); every other RdRp run printed none.
    VP1   4 axes: s17 0.692/0.964, s19 0.737/0.976 (3 rounds)
          8 axes: s17 0.860/0.805, s19 0.866/0.749
         32 axes: s19 0.909/0.623
         64 axes: s17 0.936/0.544
    VP1 trades homogeneity for completeness steadily as the axes double; no rung stands out.
  Agreement, rounds 5-8 where both partitions exist (adjusted Rand index, median):
    VP1 same seed, 8 vs 4 axes: 0.584 (7 pairs; homogeneity 0.989, completeness 0.651: 8 refines 4)
    VP1 between seeds: 4 axes 0.938 (3 pairs), 8 axes 0.781 (4 pairs); 16-64: no pairs
    RdRp between seeds: 8 axes 0.876 (2 pairs); nothing else
  So on the VP1 subsample what agreement there is favours the coarsest rung, where homogeneity is
  lowest -- the opposite of full-corpus RdRp -- but it rests on 3-7 pairs from two seeds, and 16
  and 32 axes, where full RdRp peaked, have no pairs at all.  Not a test of the criterion.

Final results, 30 of 30 runs (runs finished 22:18; subsample-compare into final/)
  vp1s300.fixed64.s19 finished (3118s): 8 rounds, 37-54 clusters, kept valleys at 2.6-4.2% (z 3.2-3.9);
  0.920/0.541.  Tally: RdRp 1 of 15 runs finished, VP1 6 of 15; no run at 16 axes finished on either.
  Added to the partial results:
    VP1 same seed, 64 vs 32 axes: 0.722 (4 pairs; homogeneity 0.963, completeness 0.832)
    VP1 between seeds, 64 axes: 0.859 (4 pairs)
  VP1 between seeds is 0.938 at 4 axes, 0.781 at 8 and 0.859 at 64, the labels scoring 0.69/0.96,
  0.86/0.78 and 0.93/0.54 there: reproducibility is high at both ends of the ladder, so on this
  subsample it does not single out a rung.  Still 3-7 pairs per figure and nothing at 16 or 32.
  The inline comparison at the end of run.out used a lower median for even counts; final/ is the
  one to read.
